{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-90694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Since $2005$, the population of a certain town grows at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function \n \n\n$$\nr(t)=200{e}^{0.2t}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in people per year, and $t>0$ is the time in years since $2005.$\n\nTo the nearest integer, by how many people did the population of the town increase between the years $2010$ and $2015?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-90694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given the **rate of change** in population $r(t).$ To find the change in  population between  $2010$ and $2015$, we integrate $r(t)$ between the$t=5$ and $t=10.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{10}200{e}^{0.2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =200\\cdot {{e}^{0.2t}|}_{5}^{10} \\\\  & =1\\,000\\,{{e}^{0.2t}|}_{5}^{10} \\\\  & =1\\,000({e}^{2}-{e}^{1}) \\\\  & ≈4\\,671\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.  \n\nTherefore, between  $2010$ and $2015$ the population of the town increased by $4\\,671$ people." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-90694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "050624a9064f3c350955243866a077dbb8ec512b7eb013f52418444a637dce2b"} {"question" "q-90694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-90694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$1\\,467$ people"] ["text" "$1\\,764$ people"] ["text" "$4\\,671$ people"] ["text" "$6\\,471$ people"] ["text" "$7\\,461$ people"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-90694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$4\\,671$ people"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-62799" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A water tank is filling with water. The rate $r(t)$ at which water enters the tank is given by the function \n\n$$\nr(t)=20{e}^{-2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in liters per minute, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes since the tank started filling up. \n\nTo one decimal place, how much water enters the tank between $t=1$ minute and $t=3$ minutes?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-62799" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given the **rate of change** of volume $r(t).$ To find the **net change** in volume between the times $t=1$ and $t=3,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}20{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={-\\frac{20}{2}{e}^{-2t}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & ={-10{e}^{-2t}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-10({e}^{-6}-{e}^{-2}) \\\\  & ≈1.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place. \n\nTherefore, between $t=1$ minute and $t=3$ minutes, $1.3$ liters of water entered the tank." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-62799" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "050624a9064f3c350955243866a077dbb8ec512b7eb013f52418444a637dce2b"} {"question" "q-62799" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-62799" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$0.9$ liters"] ["text" "$1.3$ liters"] ["text" "$1.5$ liters"] ["text" "$1.6$ liters"] ["text" "$2.2$ liters"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-62799" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$1.3$ liters"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349771" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A website is tracking the rate at which new users sign up. The rate $r(t)$ at which users sign up is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=12\\cdot {2}^{t/3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in users per day, and $t>0$ is the time in days.\n\nBetween $t=3$ and $t=9$ days, to nearest integer,\n{{field-1}} users signed up." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349771" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We are given the signup rate $r(t)$ in users per day. To find the number of users that signed up between the times $t=3$ and $t=9$ days, we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{9}12\\cdot {2}^{t/3}\\,\\text{d}t & ={12\\cdot \\frac{3\\cdot {2}^{t/3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}|}_{3}^{9} \\\\  & ={36\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{t/3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}|}_{3}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({2}^{3}-{2}^{1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{216}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & ≈312\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer. \n\nTherefore, between $t=3$ and $t=9$ days, $312$ users signed up." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349771" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "050624a9064f3c350955243866a077dbb8ec512b7eb013f52418444a637dce2b"} {"question" "q-349771" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349771" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "312"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349765" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A concert venue is tracking the rate at which guests enter the building. The rate $r(t)$ at which guests arrive is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\sqrt{t+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in guests per minute, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes.\n\nBetween $t=0$ and $t=3$ minutes, to one decimal place,\n{{field-1}} guests arrived." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349765" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given the **rate of change** in the number of guests, $r(t).$ To find the number of guests that arrived between the times $t=0$ and $t=3$ minutes, we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}3\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =3\\,\\frac{2(t+1)^{3/2}}{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =2(t+1)^{3/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =2(3+1)^{3/2}-2(0+1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =2(4)^{3/2}-2(1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =16-2 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, between $t=0$ and $t=3$ minutes, $14$ guests arrived." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349765" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "050624a9064f3c350955243866a077dbb8ec512b7eb013f52418444a637dce2b"} {"question" "q-349765" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349765" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "14.0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A laboratory is tracking the rate at which a chemical is being produced in a reaction. The rate $r(t)$ at which the chemical is produced is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in milligrams per hour, and $0<t<5$ is the time in hours since $1\\,\\text{pm}.$\n\nBetween $t=1$ and $t=3$ hours,\n{{field-1}} milligrams were produced." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given the **rate of change** of chemical production $r(t).$ To find the **net amount** of chemical produced by the reaction between the times $t=1$ and $t=3,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t & ={(\\frac{2}{4}{t}^{4}-\\frac{3}{3}{t}^{3}+4t)|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}-{t}^{3}+4t)|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}(3)^{4}-(3)^{3}+4(3))-(\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{4}-(1)^{3}+4(1)) \\\\  & =(\\frac{81}{2}-27+12)-(\\frac{1}{2}-1+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{2}-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, between $t=1$ and $t=3$ hours, $22$ milligrams were produced." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "050624a9064f3c350955243866a077dbb8ec512b7eb013f52418444a637dce2b"} {"question" "q-349752" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349752" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "22"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-62825" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A tank has an inlet and an outlet. When the inlet is turned on, the rate at which water flows into the tank governed by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=9{t}^{2}+18,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in liters per minute and $t>0$ is the time in minutes.\n\nWhen the outlet is switched on, water leaves the tank through it at a rate of $14$ liters per minute.\n\nThe inlet is switched on, and the water level increases to $120$ liters, at which point the outlet is also switched on. How much water is in the tank $10$ minutes after switching on the outlet, given that the outlet is switched on at $t=0?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-62825" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Water enters the tank at a rate of $9{t}^{2}+18$ liters per minute and leaves at a rate of $14$ liters per minute. Therefore, the **rate of change of volume** (call it $R(t)$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-14 \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}+18-14 \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total volume** of water in the tank after $t$ minutes (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =120+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(9{x}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nTo find out the volume of water after $10$ minutes, we substitute $t=10$ into the above and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(10) & =120+{\\int }_{0}^{10}(9{x}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =120+{[3{x}^{3}+4x]|}_{0}^{10} \\\\  & =120+[(3(10)^{3}+4(10))-(0)] \\\\  & =120+3040 \\\\  & =3\\,160\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-62825" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "050624a9064f3c350955243866a077dbb8ec512b7eb013f52418444a637dce2b"} {"question" "q-62825" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-62825" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$250$ liters"] ["text" "$350$ liters"] ["text" "$3\\,020$ liters"] ["text" "$3\\,140$ liters"] ["text" "$3\\,160$ liters"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-62825" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$3\\,160$ liters"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-62808" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Air is being pumped into a tire. The rate $r(t)$ at which air enters the tire is given by a function\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\frac{500}{1+2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in ${\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min},$ and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. Initially, there was $50\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}$ of air in the tire.\n\nWhich integral gives the volume of air in the tire $t$ minutes since the pumping started?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-62808" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given the **rate of change of volume** $r(t).$ To find the **total volume** after $t$ minutes (call it $V(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(t) & =V(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{500}{1+2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =50+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{500}{1+2x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that our final function is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-62808" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "050624a9064f3c350955243866a077dbb8ec512b7eb013f52418444a637dce2b"} {"question" "q-62808" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-62808" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "50+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{500}{1+2x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{50}(50+\\frac{500}{1+2x})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{50}\\frac{500}{1+2x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{t}(50+\\frac{500}{1+2x})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{500}{1+2x}\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-62808" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "50+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{500}{1+2x}\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-62803" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In a particular business bank account, the rate at which money is being deposited into the account is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=600(2t+1)^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in dollars per month, and $t>0$ is the number of months since the account was opened. When the account was first opened, the business owners placed an initial deposit of $$1\\,000$ into the account.\n\nHow much money will be in the account after $3$ months?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-62803" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given a **rate of change** in the amount of money $r(t).$ \n\nTo find the **total amount** of money in the account after $t$ months (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}r(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+{\\int }_{0}^{t}600(2x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo be sure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nWe now let $t=3,$ and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3) & =1\\,000+{\\int }_{0}^{3}600(2x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+600{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+\\frac{600}{2\\cdot 4}{(2t+1)^{4}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000+75{(2t+1)^{4}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[(2(3)+1)^{4}-(2(0)+1)^{4}] \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[{7}^{4}-{1}^{4}] \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[2\\,401-1] \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[2\\,400] \\\\  & =181\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there will be $$181\\,000$ in the account after $3$ months." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-62803" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "050624a9064f3c350955243866a077dbb8ec512b7eb013f52418444a637dce2b"} {"question" "q-62803" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-62803" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$$176\\,000$"] ["text" "$$181\\,000$"] ["text" "$$185\\,000$"] ["text" "$$291\\,000$"] ["text" "$$402\\,000$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-62803" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$$181\\,000$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Engineers are testing a water pump filling a reservoir.\nThey observe that water enters the reservoir at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=12\\sqrt{t+4},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in gallons per minute, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes since the pump was turned on.\nAt the same time, water leaks from the reservoir at a constant rate of $6$ gallons per minute.\n\nIf the reservoir was initially empty, find the expression that gives $V(t),$ the volume of water in the reservoir $t$ minutes after the pump was turned on.\n\n$V(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,6t-64$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Water is entering the reservoir at a rate of $r(t)$ gallons per minute and is leaking at a rate of $6$ gallons per minute. Therefore, the **rate of change** $R(t)$ of the volume of water is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-6 \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{t+4}-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total volume $V(t)$ after $t$ minutes, we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(t) & =V(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}(12\\sqrt{x+4}-6)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that our final function is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. Also, since the reservoir is initially empty, we set $V(0)=0.$\n\nWe can simplify the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}12\\sqrt{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{t}6\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={12\\cdot \\frac{2(x+4)^{3/2}}{3}|}_{0}^{t}-6x{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =8(t+4)^{3/2}-8(4)^{3/2}-6t \\\\  & =8(t+4)^{3/2}-6t-64\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "050624a9064f3c350955243866a077dbb8ec512b7eb013f52418444a637dce2b"} {"question" "q-349982" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349982" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8(t+4)^{3/2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349983" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A zoo is tracking the number of penguins in a habitat.\nThey observe that new penguins arrive at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=30{e}^{-t/2},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in penguins per month, and $t>0$ is the time in months since the tracking began.\nAt the same time, $3$ penguins are transferred out each month.\n\nIf there were initially $200$ penguins in the habitat, find the expression that gives $P(t),$ the number of penguins in the habitat $t$ months after the tracking began.\n\n$P(t)=-60{e}^{-t/2}\\,+$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349983" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The number of penguins is increasing at a rate of $r(t)$ penguins per month and is decreasing at a rate of $3$ penguins per month. Therefore, the **rate of change** $R(t)$ of the number of penguins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-3 \\\\  & =30{e}^{-t/2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total number $P(t)$ of penguins after $t$ months, we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =200+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(30{e}^{-x/2}-3)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that our final function is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration.\n\nWe can simplify the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t) & =200+{\\int }_{0}^{t}30{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{t}3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =200+(-60{e}^{-x/2}){|}_{0}^{t}-3x{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =200+(60-60{e}^{-t/2})-3t \\\\  & =-60{e}^{-t/2}+260-3t\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349983" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "050624a9064f3c350955243866a077dbb8ec512b7eb013f52418444a637dce2b"} {"question" "q-349983" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349983" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "260-3t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-90756" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A man stocked a pond with $250$ fish. After $t$ weeks, the number of fish in the pond is given by the function \n\n$$\nF(t)=250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in weeks) since the man put the fish in the pond.\n\nAt what time is the number of fish in the pond at a minimum, and how many fish are there at that time?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-90756" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find when the number of fish in the pond is at minimum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8t-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n8t-24=0\\,⟹\\,t=3\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=3.$ To find out how many fish are when $t=3,$ we figure out $F(3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(3) & =250+{\\int }_{0}^{3}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =250{[4{x}^{2}-24x]|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =250+[(4(3)^{2}-24(3))-(0)] \\\\  & =250-36 \\\\  & =214\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $214<250$, we conclude that the minimum number of fish in the pond is $214$, and this will happen after $3$ weeks." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-90756" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "050624a9064f3c350955243866a077dbb8ec512b7eb013f52418444a637dce2b"} {"question" "q-90756" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-90756" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The minimum is $214$ fish after $3$ weeks"] ["text" "The minimum is $215$ fish after $4$ weeks"] ["text" "The minimum is $218$ fish after $2$ weeks"] ["text" "The minimum is $230$ fish after $1$ week"] ["text" "The minimum is $230$ fish after $5$ weeks"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-90756" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The minimum is $214$ fish after $3$ weeks"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "On a certain afternoon, the total number of people $N(t)$ visiting the city's zoo is given by the function\n\n$$\nN(t)=50+5{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6-2x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since noon.  \n\nAt what time is the number of people in the zoo at a maximum, and how many people are at the zoo at that time?\n\nThe maximum number of people is\n{{field-1}}\nafter\n{{field-2}}\nhours." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find when the number of people at the zoo is at maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(50+5{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(50)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5(6-2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n5(6-2t)=0\\,⟹\\,t=3\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=3.$ To find out how many people are in the zoo when $t=3,$ we figure out $N(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(3) & =50+5{\\int }_{0}^{3}(6-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =50+5{[6x-{x}^{2}]|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =50+5[(6\\cdot 3-{3}^{2})-(0)] \\\\  & =50+5\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $50<95$, we conclude that the maximum number of people at the zoo is $95$, and this will happen after $3$ hours." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "050624a9064f3c350955243866a077dbb8ec512b7eb013f52418444a637dce2b"} {"question" "q-349998" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349998" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "95"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349998" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349998" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-62828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The total number of people $T(t)$ at a festival after $t$ hours is given by the function\n\n$$\nT(t)=10+{\\int }_{0}^{t}2x-4\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is measured in thousands of people, and $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since the festival opened its gates.\n\nAt what time is the number of people at the festival a minimum, and how many people are at the festival at this time?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-62828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find when the number of people is at a minimum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+{\\int }_{0}^{t}2x-4\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}2x-4\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}2x-4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2t-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n2t-4=0\\,⟹\\,t=2\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=2.$ To find out how many people are in the festival when $t=2,$ we figure out $T(2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =10+{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x-4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10+{[{x}^{2}-4x]|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =10+[({2}^{2}-4(2))-(0)] \\\\  & =10-4 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $6\\,000<10\\,000$, we conclude that the minimum number of people is $6\\,000$, and this will happen after $2$ hours." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-62828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "050624a9064f3c350955243866a077dbb8ec512b7eb013f52418444a637dce2b"} {"question" "q-62828" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-62828" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The minimum is $10\\,000$ people after $0$ hours"] ["text" "The minimum is $4\\,000$ people after $5$ hours"] ["text" "The minimum is $5\\,000$ people after $4$ hours"] ["text" "The minimum is $6\\,000$ people after $2$ hours"] ["text" "The minimum is $8\\,000$ people after $2$ hours"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-62828" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The minimum is $6\\,000$ people after $2$ hours"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349996" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "On a certain afternoon, the total number of people $N(t)$ visiting the city's zoo is given by the function\n\n$$\nN(t)=60+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since noon.  \n\nAt what time is the number of people in the zoo at a maximum, and how many people are at the zoo at that time?\n\nThe maximum number of people is\n{{field-1}}\nafter\n{{field-2}}\nhours." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349996" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find when the number of people at the zoo is at maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(60+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(60)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n48-3{t}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,t=4\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=4.$ To find out how many people are in the zoo when $t=4,$ we figure out $N(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(4) & =60+{\\int }_{0}^{4}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =60+{[48x-{x}^{3}]|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =60+[(48\\cdot 4-{4}^{3})-(0)] \\\\  & =60+[192-64] \\\\  & =60+128 \\\\  & =188\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $60<188$, we conclude that the maximum number of people at the zoo is $188$, and this will happen after $4$ hours." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349996" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "050624a9064f3c350955243866a077dbb8ec512b7eb013f52418444a637dce2b"} {"question" "q-349996" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349996" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "188"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349996" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-349996" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-90758" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The total number of people $V(t)$ who have visited a certain shopping center after $t$ hours is given by the function\n\n$$\nV(t)=1\\,000+100{\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since the shopping center opened its doors to the public.\n\nAt what time is the number of people visiting the shopping center at a minimum, and how many people are in the shopping center at that time?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-90758" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find when the number of people is at minimum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1\\,000+100{\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1\\,000)+100\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =100\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =100(2t-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n100(2t-6)=0\\,⟹\\,t=3\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=3.$ To find out how many people are in the shopping center when $t=3,$ we figure out $V(3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(3) & =1\\,000+100{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+100{[{x}^{2}-6x]|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000+100[({3}^{2}-6(3))-(0)] \\\\  & =1\\,000-900 \\\\  & =100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $100<1\\,000$, we conclude that the minimum number of people is $100$, and this will happen after $3$ hours." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-90758" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "050624a9064f3c350955243866a077dbb8ec512b7eb013f52418444a637dce2b"} {"question" "q-90758" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-90758" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The minimum is $100$ people after $3$ hours"] ["text" "The minimum is $150$ people after $5$ hours"] ["text" "The minimum is $200$ people after $2$ hours"] ["text" "The minimum is $200$ people after $4$ hours"] ["text" "The minimum is $500$ people after $1$ hour"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "q-90758" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The minimum is $100$ people after $3$ hours"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ad05d9ee6ce20d250afd4cf1670c0136b1a63abd281181c633480c1c847ded9"} {"question" "e-3892" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "At a shopping mall, the rate at which people are entering the mall is given by\n\n$$\nr(t) = 300t^2+50,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in people per hour, and $t>0$ is the number of hours since the mall opened. In addition, people are leaving the mall at a steady rate of $25$ people per hour. \n\n- Calculate the number of people that entered the mall in the first two hours.\n\n- Calculate the net change in the number of people inside the mall in the first 2 hours." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/example-3892.json" "file_sha256" "5acaacbbc42816ce1a086d495ac1d5380e31ff7d77e46ea33664d50243bcd5f1"} {"question" "e-3892" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Part 1**\n\nPeople are entering the mall at a rate of $r(t) = 300t^2+50.$ To find out how many people entered the mall between $t=0$ and $t=2,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}300{t}^{2}+50\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={100{t}^{3}+50t|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(100\\cdot {2}^{3}+50(2))-(0) \\\\  & =(800+100) \\\\  & =900\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Part 2**\n\nPeople are entering the mall at a rate of $r(t) = 300t^2+50$ people per hour, and are leaving the mall at a rate of $25$ people per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of people inside the mall, which we'll call $R(t),$ is given by:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **net change** in the number of people, we integrate $R(t)$ between $t=0$ and $t=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}R(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}r(t)-25\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(t)\\,\\text{d}t}_{⏟}}_{900}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}25\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =900-[25t]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =900-50 \\\\  & =850\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, over the first two hours, there was a net increase of $850$ people." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/example-3892.json" "file_sha256" "5acaacbbc42816ce1a086d495ac1d5380e31ff7d77e46ea33664d50243bcd5f1"} {"question" "e-3893" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The rate at which guests are arriving at a restaurant one evening is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t) = 6t^2+2,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in guests per hour and $t > 0$ is the time in hours since the restaurant opened. Guests are also leaving at a steady rate of $6$ guests per hour. If there were $50$ guests in the restaurant when it opened, how many guests will be in the restaurant after $1$ hour?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/example-3893.json" "file_sha256" "9681a07d889459c16530e0cfe0294e70d52eafe379f6ed41895aa8cad4472edd"} {"question" "e-3893" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Guests are entering the restaurant at a rate of $6t^2+2$ guests per hour and are leaving at a rate of $6$ guests per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of guests (call it $R(t)$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-6 \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}+2-6 \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total number of guests** after $t$ hours (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =50+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nTo find out the number of guests after $1$ hour, we substitute $t=1$ into the above and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =50+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =50+{[2{x}^{3}-4x]|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =50+[(2(1)^{3}-4(1))-(0)] \\\\  & =50-2 \\\\  & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/example-3893.json" "file_sha256" "9681a07d889459c16530e0cfe0294e70d52eafe379f6ed41895aa8cad4472edd"} {"question" "e-3894" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In example 2, we saw that the number of guests in a restaurant is given by\n\n$$\nT(t) = 50 + \\int_0^t \\left(6x^2-4\\right)\\,\\textrm d x\\,.\n$$\n\nGiven that $T(t)$ has a local minimum for some $t>0,$ find the time when the number of guests is at its minimum value." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/example-3894.json" "file_sha256" "572fe868395202738ffff0a44e2672ae773a09c40a4f84ea33584e5c75621d9b"} {"question" "e-3894" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the time when the number of guests is minimized, we need to solve $\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = 0.$\n\nDifferentiating $T(t)$ with respect to $t$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(50+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(50)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n6t^2-4 = 0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad t = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\nSo we have $2$ critical points $t=\\sqrt{\\dfrac 2 3}$ and $t=0$ (the endpoint). But since the local minimum exists for $t>0,$ $t=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{3}}$ must be the minimum value." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058280/example-3894.json" "file_sha256" "572fe868395202738ffff0a44e2672ae773a09c40a4f84ea33584e5c75621d9b"}] "usage" {"q-62828" [] "e-3893" [] "e-3894" [] "e-3892" [] "q-62799" []} "basis" 1871 "retractions" [[17592186147954 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA water tank is filling with water. The rate $r(t)$ at which water enters the tank is given by the function\n\n$r(t) = 20e^{-2t}$,\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in liters per minute, and $t > 0$ is the time in minutes since the tank started filling up.\n\nTo one decimal place, how much water enters the tank between $t = 1$ minute and $t = 3$ minutes?"] [17592186147963 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe total number of people $T(t)$ at a festival after $t$ hours is given by the function\n\n$T(t) = 10 + ∫_{0}^{t}2x - 4dx$,\n\nwhere $T$ is measured in thousands of people, and $t > 0$ is the time (in hours) since the festival opened its gates.\n\nAt what time is the number of people at the festival a minimum, and how many people are at the festival at this time?"] [17592186147276 :question/problem "At a shopping mall, the rate at which people are entering the mall is given by\n\n$r(t) = 300t^2+50$,\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in people per hour, and $t>0$ is the number of hours since the mall opened. In addition, people are leaving the mall at a steady rate of $25$ people per hour.\n\n1. Calculate the number of people that entered the mall in the first two hours.\n2. Calculate the net change in the number of people inside the mall in the first 2 hours."] [17592186147276 :question/worked-solution "**Part 1**\n\nPeople are entering the mall at a rate of\n\n$$\nr(t) = 300t^2+50\n$$\n\nTo find out how many people entered the mall between $t=0$ and $t=2$, we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{2}r(t)dt &= ∫_{0}^{2}300t^{2} + 50dt \\\\\n&= 100t^{3} + 50t \\mid _{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= (100 \\cdot 2^{3} + 50(2)) - (0) \\\\\n&= (800 + 100) \\\\\n&= 900\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Part 2**\n\nPeople are entering the mall at a rate of\n\n$$\nr(t) = 300t^2+50\n$$\n\npeople per hour, and are leaving the mall at a rate of $25$ people per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of people inside the mall, which we'll call $R(t)$, is given by:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(t) &= r(t) - 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **net change** in the number of people, we integrate $R(t)$ between $t=0$ and $t=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{2}R(t)dt &= ∫_{0}^{2}r(t) - 25dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}r(t)dt_(⏟)_(900) - ∫_{0}^{2}25dt \\\\\n&= 900 - [25t]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= 900 - 50 \\\\\n&= 850\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, over the first two hours, there was a net increase of $850$ people."] [17592186147277 :question/problem "The rate at which guests are arriving at a restaurant one evening is given by the function\n\n$r(t) = 6t^2+2$,\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in guests per hour and $t > 0$ is the time in hours since the restaurant opened. Guests are also leaving at a steady rate of $6$ guests per hour. If there were $50$ guests in the restaurant when it opened, how many guests will be in the restaurant after $1$ hour?"] [17592186147277 :question/worked-solution "Guests are entering the restaurant at a rate of $6t^2+2$ guests per hour and are leaving at a rate of $6$ guests per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of guests (call it $R(t)$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(t) &= r(t) - 6 \\\\\n&= 6t^{2} + 2 - 6 \\\\\n&= 6t^{2} - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total number of guests** after $t$ hours (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(t) &= T(0) + ∫_{0}^{t}R(x)dx \\\\\n&= 50 + ∫_{0}^{t}(6x^{2} - 4)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration.\n\nTo find out the number of guests after $1$ hour, we substitute $t=1$ into the above and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(1) &= 50 + ∫_{0}^{1}(6x^{2} - 4)dx \\\\\n&= 50 + [2x^{3} - 4x] \\mid _{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 50 + [(2(1)^{3} - 4(1)) - (0)] \\\\\n&= 50 - 2 \\\\\n&= 48\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186147278 :question/problem "In example 2, we saw that the number of guests in a restaurant is given by\n\n$T(t) = 50 + \\int_0^t \\left(6x^2-4\\right)\\,\\textrm d x\\,$.\n\nGiven that $T(t)$ has a local minimum for some $t>0$, find the time when the number of guests is at its minimum value."] [17592186147278 :question/worked-solution "To find the time when the number of guests is minimized, we need to solve\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = 0\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $T(t)$ with respect to $t$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dT}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(50 + ∫_{0}^{t}(6x^{2} - 4)dx) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(50) + \\frac{d}{dt}∫_{0}^{t}(6x^{2} - 4)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}∫_{0}^{t}(6x^{2} - 4)dx \\\\\n&= 6t^{2} - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = 0\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n6t^2-4 = 0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad t = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\nSo we have $2$ critical points\n\n$$\nt=\\sqrt{\\dfrac 2 3}\n$$\n\nand $t=0$ (the endpoint). But since the local minimum exists for $t>0$,\n\n$$\nt=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\nmust be the minimum value."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186145736 17592186145737 17592186145738 17592186145739 17592186145740 17592186145790 17592186145791 17592186145792 17592186145793 17592186145794] "immutable_fields" [[17592186145735 :answer-field/correct] [17592186145735 :answer-field/id] [17592186145735 :answer-field/key] [17592186145735 :answer-field/type] [17592186145789 :answer-field/correct] [17592186145789 :answer-field/id] [17592186145789 :answer-field/key] [17592186145789 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
